%%newpage
%%textfont Times-Bold 20
%%center Schottisches 
%%textfont Times-Roman 14

%%begintext align
%%About the middle of the C19th, a new dance made its appearance in Germany known as the schottisch, or Scottish dance.
%%The French and then the English added an 'e' to the name making it the schottische, and added a subtitle, the German polka-waltz,
%%which gives some idea of how the original dance was performed. The lively dance slowed down somewhat in our warmer climate,
%%and ultimately became the present-day barn dance.
%%-- John Meredith, Folk Songs of Australia, Vol 2, p 24
%%endtext
